@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for macOS (Apple silicon and Intel)
==========================================================

@TITLE@ (@STUDIO@), recompiled to run natively: the game's own program
translated into C++ and compiled for your Mac, drawn with Vulkan (through
MoltenVK, which comes with the app).

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the game: @RELEASE@,
dumped from the GD-ROM as a .cue file with its .bin tracks (a Redump-style
dump, music tracks included), a .gdi with its tracks, or a .chd.


Playing
-------
1. Drag @TITLE@.app onto Applications (in this disk image's window). Building
   the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open @TITLE@ from Applications. The first time, macOS stops it (the app
   isn't signed by an Apple developer account, see below): open System
   Settings, Privacy & Security, scroll down to "@TITLE@ was blocked", click
   Open Anyway and confirm with your password. macOS asks only once.
3. The game is compiled with Apple's own compiler, free from Apple (its
   licence doesn't let it come with this download). If your Mac doesn't have
   it yet, the app says so: click "Install Apple's command line tools",
   follow Apple's installer (a few minutes), then click "Check again".
4. Click "Choose your disc" and pick your disc's .cue, .gdi or .chd file,
   or drag the file onto the window. It tells you straight away if the disc
   is the wrong game or another version.
5. Wait for the first build: a few minutes. Your game files never leave your
   Mac.
6. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs.

After that, the app remembers your disc, and Play starts the game straight
away. After an update it builds the game again first, recompiling only what
changed.

The game's output is in run.log and run-errors.log, and the build's in
build.log, all in ~/Library/Application Support/qilin/@GAME@/work (in Finder:
Go, Go to Folder). Send them when reporting a problem.

About "Open Anyway": the app is signed only by itself ("ad hoc"), not by an
Apple developer account, so Gatekeeper can't name who made it. Check that you
downloaded it from the project's GitHub releases page (below) before you
open it. Nothing in it changes that check or works around it.

Needs: macOS 14.5 (Sonoma) or newer, on an Apple silicon or Intel Mac, and
Apple's command line tools from Xcode 16 or newer, which need that macOS
(the app checks; older tools are updated with System Settings, General,
Software Update). Graphics go through MoltenVK, Vulkan over Apple's Metal.


Controls
--------
A Dreamcast controller. Up to four players: the first controller (or the
keyboard) is port A, the others ports B to D.

  Controller           Keyboard        Dreamcast
  left stick           arrows          the analogue stick
  D-pad                I J K L         the D-pad
  A, B, X, Y           Z, X, A, S      A, B, X, Y
  LB, RB, triggers     Q, W            L, R
  Start                Return          Start

Back + Start together, or F1 or Esc: the settings menu (the game pauses):
controls (set by pressing them), the window, the picture, the refresh
rate, enhancements and the Dreamcast's own settings (language, sound).
Option+Return, or the window's green button: full screen. On a Mac
keyboard F1 may need the fn key. A controller that rumbles plays the Jump
Pack.

Settings and memory-card saves are kept in ~/Library/Application
Support/qilin/@GAME@, with the disc you chose, the build (work) and its cache
of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in the app
-----------------
Right-click @TITLE@.app, Show Package Contents:

Contents/MacOS/Play @TITLE@       chooses your disc, builds the game (once)
                                  and starts it
Contents/Resources/kit/qilin      the recompiler: turns the disc's @BOOT@
                                  into C++
Contents/Resources/kit/lib, include
                                  the prebuilt rest of the game: its
                                  runtime, the renderer, sound, the window
                                  and controls (SDL3), the menu (Dear ImGui)
Contents/Resources/kit/files.txt  every file this release holds (see below)
Contents/Frameworks/libMoltenVK.dylib
                                  MoltenVK: Vulkan over Metal (Khronos)
Contents/Resources/LICENSES       the licences of everything included

The game itself (@EXE@), built from your disc, is in the work folder above,
not in the app: the app is never changed by building.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/Qilin1066/ToyCommander--QilinRecomp

Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in the app asks GitHub for a newer release of @TITLE@
(it sends nothing about you or your Mac). If there is one, it downloads the
disk image, copies the new app beside this one and swaps it in, then starts
again and builds the game for the new version. If anything fails on the
way, the old version is left as it was. The app must be somewhere you can
write to for that (Applications is, for an administrator): run from the disk
image itself, it can't update. You can also drag a new download's app over
this one in Applications: it finds your disc and the cache by itself.
Your saves and settings are never in the app, so nothing of yours is
touched.

The game is compiled for this Mac's processor; on another Mac, it is built
again. Compiled files come from the cache when nothing they're made from has
changed. A failed build leaves the working game as it was; the one before a
new build is kept as work/@EXE@.previous until the new one has run and
closed normally. "Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.

Before a save burst, the memory card is copied into its five-entry history;
flash updates keep their own history too. Use the settings menu's Game page
to restore a copy and restart. VMU packets are checked small writes; the
history supports recovery after an interrupted save, rather than making a
whole game save transactional. Existing unreadable or malformed card/flash
files stop the game and are left unchanged. Keep a separate copy before
manual recovery.

Sound and controller recovery
-----------------------------
If the sound output is unavailable, the game keeps running and retries it
once a second. Playback resumes with the selected volume and mute setting;
sound from the outage is discarded rather than played late. Recovery and
errors are recorded in run-errors.log.

A controller that pressed while all four ports were full can take a port
when another controller disconnects. Existing players keep their ports.
Vibration expires quickly if the game stops updating it.
